% 1. 承上起下：尽管上述方法能够迁移语义与视觉动态知识，但将其用于人形全身控制，仍面临运动先验与动作建模两方面的挑战
% 2. 阐述问题1：人形全身运动先验不足。 相较于固定基座机械臂操作，人形全身控制需要在同一动作序列中建模更高维、强耦合的运动关系。然而，VLM/VGM 预训练主要提供语义理解与视觉动态知识，并不直接赋予动作生成模块相应的全身运动先验。因此，迁移后的动作专家仍需从有限的人形示范中学习跨关节协调与时序结构，承担较大的学习负担。
% 3. 阐述问题2: 动作差异化建模不足。 根部移动、身体姿态与手部操作具有不同的运动尺度和精度要求，而统一预测与部分解耦架构仍由共享动作专家联合建模全部或部分运动分量，未充分体现这些差异。这种建模方式可能引入不同控制目标之间的学习干扰，增加兼顾局部动作精度与全身协调的难度。
Despite these advances, transferring semantic and visual dynamics knowledge to humanoid whole-body control presents two challenges.
\textbf{\ding{182} Insufficient Whole-Body Motion Priors.}
Compared with fixed-base manipulation, humanoid control requires modeling higher-dimensional, strongly coupled motion across body parts and time within each action sequence.
However, vision--language and video pretraining primarily capture semantic information and visual dynamics, without directly providing the action generator with motion priors matched to the humanoid's kinematic representation~\cite{grootn1,psi0,motionwam,wholebodywam_wbc}.
The action expert must therefore acquire inter-joint coordination and temporal structure from limited humanoid demonstrations, placing a substantial learning burden on policy adaptation.
\textbf{\ding{183} Insufficient Differentiation of Motion Components.}
Root motion, body articulation, and hand control operate at different motion scales and require different levels of precision.
Unified action generators place these heterogeneous components within a shared prediction process, which can introduce interference between control objectives~\cite{openhlm,motionwam}.
Hybrid designs distinguish upper-body joint control from lower-body locomotion, but this division of execution responsibilities does not by itself provide specialized generation pathways for root, body, and hand motion~\cite{humanoidexo,wholebodyvla}.
These designs therefore leave substantial demands on the action predictor to accommodate component-specific requirements while learning the dependencies needed for whole-body coordination.




% 逻辑 
% 1. 提出并概述方法, 对应图 1 中部
% 2. 回应问题1，强动作先验: 选择 T2M 骨干，对应图 2(c)
% 3. 回应问题2, 强: 3阶段框架, 对应图 2(f)
% 4. 概述取得的效果, 对应图 1 顶部

Recent advances in text-to-motion (T2M) generation provide structured motion priors that offer a foundation for addressing these limitations~\cite{temos,mdm,mld,t2mgpt,momask,omnicontrol,kimodo}.
Building on these advances, we present \textbf{Kimodo-Policy}, a humanoid VLA model built on a pretrained T2M backbone with a three-stage root--body--hand hierarchy.
We pretrain the VLA policy on diverse humanoid demonstrations to map visual observations, language instructions, and motion history to coordinated whole-body actions (Fig.~1).
\textbf{\ding{182} Strong Whole-Body Motion Priors.}
We transfer Kimodo's pretrained motion generator into the VLA framework (Fig.~2(c)), equipping action generation with priors over inter-joint coordination and temporal structure~\cite{kimodo}.
These priors support high-dimensional action prediction and allow VLA training to focus on grounding established motion capabilities in visual observations and task instructions, easing the burden of learning motion regularities from limited humanoid demonstrations.
\textbf{\ding{183} Strong Component-Specific Motion Modeling.}
We extend the pretrained root--body structure with a dedicated hand-control module, forming a three-stage hierarchy with explicit cross-stage conditioning (Fig.~2(f)).
The root stage predicts global translation and heading; the body stage generates articulated motion conditioned on the predicted root trajectory; and the hand stage predicts hand controls conditioned on visual observations and future body features.
This architecture combines component-specific generation with explicit conditional dependencies to support coordinated whole-body behavior.
With only 678M total parameters and 92M trainable parameters, Kimodo-Policy achieves strong performance on HumanoidArena, SIMPLE, and real-world loco-manipulation tasks (Fig.~1, top)~\cite{humanoidarena,simple}.
Scaling studies further show performance gains from larger VLA pretraining datasets and models, as well as improved downstream capabilities from stronger pretrained T2M priors. Our main contributions are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item We introduce \textbf{Kimodo-Policy}, a humanoid VLA framework that transfers pretrained T2M motion priors to visually guided whole-body control. Through VLA pretraining on diverse humanoid demonstrations, the policy learns to condition these priors on visual observations, language instructions, and motion history for closed-loop execution.

    \item We extend the pretrained root--body structure into a three-stage root--body--hand policy with dedicated hand control. This hierarchy provides specialized generation pathways for distinct motion components while explicitly modeling their conditional dependencies to support whole-body coordination.

    \item We demonstrate strong performance on HumanoidArena, SIMPLE, and real-world humanoid tasks with 678M total parameters and only 92M trainable parameters. Scaling studies further establish that policy performance improves with larger VLA pretraining datasets and model capacity, and that stronger pretrained T2M models translate into more capable downstream VLA policies.
\end{itemize}
